# With `bun checkfu deploy`, a variable exported in your shell is used for any
# entry left empty here, and an entry with a value here wins over the shell. The
# exception is CHECKFU_API_KEY: if both set it to different keys, the deploy
# stops, because later commands would send the shell's key. `bun run dev`,
# `bun run deploy` and `bun run deploy:production` run Alchemy directly, and
# there a value exported in your shell wins over this file.

# Secret clients send in `Authorization: Bearer <key>` or `x-api-key: <key>`.
# Leave it empty and `bun checkfu deploy` generates one, or run: openssl rand -hex 32
CHECKFU_API_KEY=

# Set at least one model provider. Built-in providers:
#   - OpenAI:               OPENAI_API_KEY              (e.g. gpt-6.2-sol)
#   - Anthropic:            ANTHROPIC_API_KEY           (e.g. claude-sonnet-5-5)
#   - OpenRouter:           OPENROUTER_API_KEY          (e.g. openrouter/anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5)
#   - Cloudflare AI Gateway: CLOUDFLARE_GATEWAY_ID      (e.g. cloudflare-ai-gateway/claude-sonnet-5-5)
#       On deploy, CLOUDFLARE_GATEWAY_ID alone uses the Worker's native AI binding (zero token).
#       For local `bun run dev` or explicit HTTPS auth, also set CLOUDFLARE_AI_API_KEY and CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID.
#   - Cloudflare Workers AI: CLOUDFLARE_AI_API_KEY + CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
#       (e.g. cloudflare-workers-ai/@cf/deepseek-ai/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017)
#       (CLOUDFLARE_AI_API_KEY is separate from Alchemy's deployment CLOUDFLARE_API_KEY/CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN.)
OPENAI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
CLOUDFLARE_GATEWAY_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_AI_API_KEY=
# Uncomment to set CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID in .env (left commented so an empty
# entry here never masks an exported CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID in CI deploys).
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=

# Optional. Declare custom format-compatible providers speaking openai-responses,
# openai-completions, or anthropic-messages. Never place API keys inside this JSON;
# reference environment variable names prefixed with CHECKFU_PROVIDER_SECRET_ via
# `api_key_env`, `auth`, or `secret_headers`, and export those variables separately so
# only PiHarnessWorker receives them.
CHECKFU_PROVIDERS=
# Uncomment to configure a custom provider (overrides the empty default above):
# CHECKFU_PROVIDERS='{"xai":{"name":"xAI","format":"openai-responses","base_url":"https://api.x.ai/v1","api_key_env":"CHECKFU_PROVIDER_SECRET_XAI_API_KEY","default_model":"grok-4"}}'

# Optional. Where Durable Objects keep their data. Decide before the first
# deploy: an object inside a jurisdiction is a different object from the one
# with the same name outside it, so changing CHECKFU_JURISDICTION later starts
# from empty. Artifact files go to an R2 bucket in the same jurisdiction, so
# changing it also replaces that bucket and discards every artifact's files,
# while the objects in the old jurisdiction keep their records of them. To
# change it on a stage that holds artifacts, run
# `bun checkfu destroy --stage <name>` first, then deploy with the new value.
# That deletes the whole deployment and all of its data, not just the artifact
# bucket. The production stage refuses the change instead. R2 has no fedramp-high, so the deploy refuses it.
# CHECKFU_LOCATION_HINT only affects objects created after it is set.
# The D1 database behind the list endpoints is placed in the same jurisdiction;
# D1 has no fedramp-high, so a deploy with it stops. It ignores the location
# hint, because Alchemy replaces a D1 database whose hint changes, so D1 places
# it near wherever the first deploy runs: deploy from a machine near your users.
# CHECKFU_JURISDICTION: eu | fedramp | fedramp-high | us
# CHECKFU_LOCATION_HINT: wnam | enam | sam | weur | eeur | apac | apac-ne | apac-se | oc | afr | me
CHECKFU_JURISDICTION=
CHECKFU_LOCATION_HINT=

# Optional. Also serve the API and the MCP server on your own hostnames, for
# example api.example.com and mcp.example.com; the two must differ. Each
# hostname's zone has to be in the Cloudflare account you deploy to; the deploy
# creates the DNS record and certificate and reports these addresses. The
# workers.dev addresses keep working too, with the same API key.
# A hostname set here replaces every custom hostname on that Worker, including
# any attached in the Cloudflare dashboard. Set none to detach them all. Left
# empty here and unset in your shell, hostnames that are already attached stay
# as they are.
# To change a hostname that is already set, set it to none and deploy, then set
# the new one and deploy again. Changing it in one step detaches the old one
# first, so if the new one cannot be attached (a typo, its zone is not in the
# account, or another Worker has it, as in swapping the two) the Worker is left
# with neither.
CHECKFU_API_DOMAIN=
CHECKFU_MCP_DOMAIN=

# Optional. A domain of its own for published artifacts, such as example-pages.com:
# each one is served at https://<address>.example-pages.com/ instead of under /p/<address>/
# on the content Worker, so pages that load files from / work. Give it its own
# zone, apart from the API's, so published pages never share a registrable domain
# with the API, and use the zone apex in the Cloudflare account you deploy to;
# Cloudflare's free certificate covers only one level below it. The deploy adds a
# wildcard route and a proxied * DNS record. While it is set, CHECKFU_API_DOMAIN
# and CHECKFU_MCP_DOMAIN must each be a hostname outside it or none: left empty,
# a hostname already attached would stay, and it could be inside this domain.
# `bun run dev` creates no route and keeps publications under /p/<address>/.
CHECKFU_PUBLISH_DOMAIN=
